  "query_preview": "southward to I-80 in southern NE. An upper level trough was\nlocated off the Pacific Northwest coast. A broad upper ridge \nextended from FL northward across the mid Atlantic States. The H5 \nridge axis extended westward across the Gulf into the far southwest \nUS.\n\nThe 6Z surface map showed a cold front extending from central MN, \nsouthwest across northeast NE, then southwest into northeast CO. \nAhead of the cold front there was a line of thunderstorms across \neast central and south central NE. These storms have formed an OFB \nthat has pushed south of I-80.\n\nToday through Sunday night:\n\nThe H5 trough across the northern and central Plains will gradually \nshift east across the upper Midwest into the Great Lakes States by \nSunday night. \n\nThe surface front and OFB across southern NE will shift southward \nthrough the morning hours and scattered showers and thunderstorms \nmay develop along and north of the boundary. The 6Z Meso analysis \nshows that most of the CWA has less than 500 J/KG of MUCAPE with \n2000 J/KG of MUCAPE located across western KS. In the next 6 hours, \nthe MUCAPE will increase above 1500 J/KG across the north central \nKS, so a few storms along or north of the OFB may be strong with \ngusty winds and small hail possible. Most of the CAMs show the \nstronger thunderstorms across south central NE weakening as they push\nsoutheast into the TOP CWA after 3 AM. The NSSL WRF looks to be \ninitialized the best and provides the greater chance for showers \nthunderstorms early this morning across the CWA. The other CAMs show \nthe the line of showers and thunderstorms weakening with only widely \nscattered to isolated showers and thunderstorms through the morning \nhours.\n\nThis afternoon the HRRR, 3km NAM, and RRFS forecast the surface \nfront to stall out along I-70 this afternoon. Thunderstorms will \ndevelop along the front during the late afternoon hours, and some of \nthese storms may be severe with damaging wind gusts and large hail. \nEffective shear may increase to 35 to 45 KTS south of I-70 with \nMLCAPE 1500 to 2500 J/KG during the late afternoon hours. Most CAMs \nhave backed sfc winds along and north of the OFB that will become \nstationary across the far southern counties of the CWA. Elevated \nthunderstorms may develop north of the front across the northern \ncounties of the CWA this afternoon. These storms may produced \nquarter size hail as MUCAPES north of the front will be over 1000 \nJ/KG. Most numerical models/CAMs have the front pushing south of the \nCWA after 2Z SUN. Any severe thunderstorms will push southeast of the\nCWA during the early evening hours. There may be some weak \nisentropic lift for scattered showers and a few elevated \nthunderstorms to continue through the night\n\nSunday, the surface front will become stationary across northern OK, \nso most of the elevated thunderstorms should remain across southern \nKS. There may be some scattered showers through the day on Sunday. \nSunday night there may be a few more elevated thunderstorms as the \nsurface front retreats northward, and isentropic lift increase \nacross the CWA. The H5 trough across the Pacific Northwest will \nshift east into the central Rockies by 6Z MON.\n\n\nMonday:\n\nThe H5 trough across the central Rockies will lift northeast into the\ncentral high Plains. The surface warm front will shift northward\nacross NE into SD. The CWA will be in the warm sector. Look for a \nhot and humid day with highs in the lower to mid 90s. Heat indices \nduring the afternoon will reach 100 to 105 degrees.\n\n\nMonday night through Tuesday: \n\nThe extended range models are in good \nagreement with the H5 trough across the central high Plains lifting \nnortheast across the northern Plains on Tuesday. This will cause a \nsurface cold front to push southeast across the CWA. There may be \nscattered showers and thunderstorms along and northwest of the front.\nAt this time the stronger effective shear and instability will be \nlocated north and northeast of the CWA where the better risk for \nsevere thunderstorms will occur across eastern NE and IA. But a few \nstorms across the CWA ahead of the front may be strong to severe on \nTuesday with damaging wind gusts and large hail possible.\n\nTuesday night through Saturday:\n\nThe extended range models show the surface front becoming stationary \nacross the CWA through most of the workweek. H5 trough will lift \nnortheast from the southwest US into the Plains. There will be at \nleast a chance for showers and thunderstorms through the work week. \nBy next weekend a stronger cold front may push southeast of the CWA \nbringing an end to the rain chances. Highs will range from the upper \n70s to lower 80s north to the upper 80s to lower 90s southeast. The \ntemperature forecast will be dependent on where the front becomes \nstationary across the CWA. The stronger front moving southeast across\nthe CWA next Saturday will cool high temperatures into the 70s.",
